<Layout title="About Cosmin Ghinoiu — Client Delivery, Implementation and AI" description="Nine years at Copyright Clearance Center delivering enterprise software to pharma and life-sciences clients, 50+ projects since 2022, and an independent production AI service since October 2026." current="/about/">
  <section class="about-intro section-wrap"><div><h1>I’m Cosmin. <br />I make delivery <br />workable.</h1><a class="text-link" href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/cosmin-ghinoiu/" rel="me">Connect on LinkedIn <Arrow direction="up-right" /></a></div><div class="about-opening"><p>I do my best work when delivery is complicated: technical dependencies, competing priorities, and different teams looking at the same problem from different angles.</p><p>My role is to work out what needs to happen, turn it into a practical plan, and keep the right people moving in the same direction.</p><p>I bring that experience to client delivery, implementation and service delivery roles, permanent or contract, and to defined projects.</p></div></section>
  <section class="about-background section-wrap"><h2>From technical detail <br />to client delivery.</h2><div class="prose"><p>My background spans technical support, client engagement and enterprise implementation. I spent nine years at Copyright Clearance Center, starting as the first Romania-based support specialist for its enterprise product and finishing as Lead Client Service Delivery Manager, mostly for global pharma and life-sciences clients. That path helps me connect how the software actually works with what the people introducing, using and supporting it need from it.</p><p>Between 2022 and July 2026 I led 50+ specialized delivery projects, including implementations, integrations, migrations, technical reviews and workflow redesigns, and I was the implementation and service manager for five strategic accounts worth over $2M a year in recurring licences. Every account I managed renewed, and the contractual service levels for availability, order fulfilment and support response were met throughout. I’ve delivered over 40 API integrations, from discovery and approvals through configuration and the operational support that keeps them within the agreed terms.</p><p>I’ve also run more than 100 live user and administrator training sessions, the largest for 400+ people. Enablement has been part of the delivery work throughout, from explaining a configuration to helping a team prepare for operational ownership.</p><a class="text-link" href="/contact/">Request my current CV <Arrow /></a>
    <div class="about-certifications" id="certifications">
      <h3>Certifications</h3>
      <ul role="list">
        <li>Project Management Professional (PMP), 2026</li>
        <li>PRINCE2 Agile Foundation</li>
        <li>ITIL 4 Foundation</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div></section>
  <section class="about-ai section-wrap" id="working-with-ai"><h2>Working <br />with AI.</h2><div class="prose"><p>On the client side, I’ve delivered two enterprise AI rollouts: a 12-month pilot that went to a 20,000-user launch, and a second client’s AI search and summarization. In both, my part was the delivery and the evaluation, testing answer quality with the client, working with engineers on guardrails and retrieval settings, and keeping everything inside the content licences. The engineers built the models and the pipelines.</p><p>Since October 2026 I’ve also been running SuppClaimCheck, a production AI service that checks supplement claims against open-access literature. I designed it, I direct the Claude Code agents that write the code under rules I set, I review every change, I run the evals and I operate it. It’s the same discipline I used on the client rollouts, grounding, evaluation before rollout, constraints written down and applied in code, but with me owning every decision. Day to day I work with Claude, Claude Code, Codex and ChatGPT for research, documentation and analysis.</p><a class="text-link" href="/portfolio/#suppclaimcheck">Read about SuppClaimCheck <Arrow /></a></div></section>
  <section class="working-style section-wrap" id="working-practices"><div class="section-heading"><h2>How I work.</h2><p>Enough structure to help people execute. <br class="desktop-break" /> Clear decisions. Practical follow-through.</p></div><div class="working-rows"><article><h3>Start with the real work</h3><p>I work through the requirements, constraints and current workflows before settling on a delivery approach. The plan needs to fit the situation.</p></article><article><h3>Make ownership visible</h3><p>I connect business needs with technical reality, bring the right people into decisions, and keep actions and dependencies clear.</p></article><article><h3>Leave useful knowledge behind</h3><p>During employment, I led a weekly peer knowledge-sharing program over one year and helped develop reusable delivery and review frameworks. I bring the same attention to training, documentation and handover.</p></article><article><h3>Check it with data before trusting it</h3><p>On one account I went through more than a million user-action records with the client to work out which departments needed basic and which needed advanced training. On client AI rollouts I tested answer quality with the users before each phase. On my own service, every change runs against a labelled eval set before it can go live. I’d rather know the number than assume the result.</p></article></div></section>
